// The signature crossing that is a MISCOMPILE rather than a nominal-typing nicety, which is why the
// diagnostic names the shape and not just the two names.
//
// `f1` takes one parameter. `hp` is declared at a two-parameter signature. Before 0.9.102 the assignment
// below produced only a clang warning, and `hp(a: 10, b: 20)` then built and RAN — an indirect call
// through a mismatched function pointer, passing two arguments to a one-parameter function.
//
// ⚠️ Nothing else catches this. Named-argument checking is strict and did its job: it validated `a`/`b`
// against `H`, correctly, because `hp` is an `H`. The defect is one line earlier. And UBSan's `function`
// check — the sanitizer sub-check that exists for exactly this — is disabled suite-wide for an unrelated
// and permanent reason (run_tests.sh, and SPEC.md's sanitizer section says why).
fnptr int32 F(int32 x);
fnptr int32 H(int32 a, int32 b);

fn int32 f1(int32 x) { return x + 1; }

fn int32 main()
{
    F fp = f1;
    H hp = fp;
    return hp(a: 10, b: 20);
}
